**What**

`show` and `edit` resolve a minimal unique id prefix. `log`, `rm` and
`comment edit` do not -- they pass the raw string straight through. So the same
id string is valid for some verbs and invalid for others, and the failure modes
differ: `rm` errors, but `log` succeeds and lies.

**Where**

- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:912` -- `resolve_issue_show_id`, the resolver
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:271` and `:308` -- `Edit` and `Show` call it
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:314` -- `Log` does not
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:315` -- `Rm` does not
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:326` -- `comment edit` does not
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:902` and `:903` -- same omissions on `review`

**Reproduction**

    git-forge issue show 4cd886
    First issue (edited) #4cd8865f        # resolves

    git-forge issue log 4cd886
    issue history 4cd886:
      (none)                              # exit 0

    git-forge issue rm 4cd886
    Error: no issue 4cd886                # exit 1

The middle one is the dangerous one. It reports, with a successful exit code,
that an issue which has three commits of history has no history. A user
checking whether an edit landed would conclude it did not.

**Also**

The ambiguity error leaks literal markdown into plain terminal text:

    Error: ambiguous issue id ; matches:
      **1**d655d1a
      **46**875dbb

`emphasize_min_unique_prefix` at `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:942` wraps the
distinguishing prefix in asterisks. The intent is clearly to bold it, but this
string goes to stderr as-is and is never rendered. The tool has a `colorize`
helper eight lines away.

Relatedly, the empty string is accepted as a prefix and matches everything,
which is how the example above was produced.

**Why it matters**

Prefix ids are the right affordance and the resolver is good -- it even
computes the minimum distinguishing length. The problem is that it is bolted on
per-verb instead of being the single front door, so half the surface has it and
half does not, and nothing in the type system notices. Users learn prefixes
work, then hit a verb where they silently do not.

The `log` case is the one that would embarrass a demo, because it produces a
confident wrong answer rather than an error.

**Options**

- Option A -- Resolve every id at the argument-parsing boundary: make the id a
  newtype whose parser does the lookup, so no command can forget. Trade-off:
  needs repo access during parsing, which is awkward with `clap` derive.
- Option B -- Route all five call sites through the existing resolver.
  Trade-off: five lines, but the next command added forgets again.
- Option C -- Push resolution down into `gix-forge`, so `Issue::load`,
  `Issue::history` and `Issue::delete` all accept a prefix. Trade-off: the
  library gets an opinion about id shape, which is arguably correct since it
  also mints them.

**Recommendation**

Option C, because it puts resolution next to derivation and makes it impossible
for a new verb to differ. Option B today if C is too much churn. Also reject
the empty prefix, and either render the emphasis or drop the asterisks. High
confidence -- there is no design tension here, only inconsistency.